<section class="reference-section" id="prithu-earth"><div class="reference-section-heading"><h2>King Prithu and Mother Earth · Righteous Kingship and Prosperity</h2><a href="/prithu-earth.html">Return to story</a></div><div class="reference-content"><p><strong>Shri Vishnu Purana · Gita Press Sanskrit–Hindi illustrated edition · first part · chapter 13.</strong> This original condensed English retelling follows the owner-supplied edition. The private PDF is not copied into the public website or repository.</p><p><strong>Supplied edition:</strong> private PDF pages 57–63; printed pages 58–64. The section is titled “Raja Vena aur Prithu ka Charitra” in the Hindi contents.</p><ol><li><strong>1.13.1–24 · printed pp. 58–59</strong><small>Vena’s lineage, rejection of sacred responsibility and refusal of the sages’ counsel.</small></li><li><strong>1.13.25–40 · printed pp. 60–61</strong><small>Disorder after Vena, the sages’ intervention, Nishada and Prithu brought forth from Vena’s arm.</small></li><li><strong>1.13.41–66 · printed pp. 61–62</strong><small>Prithu’s consecration, the bards’ commission, his refusal of unearned praise and the virtues offered as standards for his reign.</small></li><li><strong>1.13.67–84 · printed pp. 62–63</strong><small>The people’s hunger, Prithu’s pursuit, the Earth’s cow form and their debate about protection and consequence.</small></li><li><strong>1.13.85–95 · printed pp. 63–64</strong><small>The agreement, levelling of the ground, Manu as calf, the recovery of seeds and herbs, and Earth receiving the name Prithvi through Prithu.</small></li></ol><p><strong>Interpretive boundary.</strong> The cow form, pursuit, dialogue, levelling of the ground, calf, vessel, seeds and medicines belong to the supplied narrative. The language of ecological stewardship and accountable prosperity is an editorial reading, clearly marked on the story page. Other Puranas expand the milking episode with different groups, calves, vessels and essences; those catalogues are not merged into this retelling.</p><p>All three images are newly AI-generated devotional interpretations. Clothing, architecture, cultivated landscape and symbolic streams of grain are imagined; they are not historical reconstructions.</p><a href="/mahapuranas.html#vishnu">Meet the Vishnu Purana</a></div></section>
